# implementation.plan — PR #1489 anomaly fixes and test hardening

## Summary and agreed decisions

Fix the three persistent CI failures and improve the anomaly module's unit, integration and E2E tests within the existing PR, using separate GPG-signed commits.
Work in the existing group-cond checkout on feat/anomaly-group-conditioning, starting from verified head e9f6fd97. Preserve unrelated changes and existing history.
No public API, profile-name, reporting-schema or model-metadata-format changes are planned. Restore the intended discovery contract without introducing another compatibility or retraining requirement.

Subsequent user clarification: retain the existing GitHub workflows and acceptance action. Extend their diagnostics, coverage retention and anomaly merge-queue conditions in place; do not add a custom action, workflow or retry-result gate. Preserve the acceptance action's current retry policy and record its limitation in the handoff.

## 1. Diagnose and repair the current failures

### Preserve actionable diagnostics first

- Add an opt-in, anomaly-scoped pytest reporting hook. Write separate sanitized reports per test, process and attempt to an absolute directory supplied through DQX_TEST_DIAGNOSTICS_DIR.
- Retain exception types, chains and available tracebacks without local variables, input rows, credentials or raw HTTP traffic. Test the reporter with synthetic failures and redaction cases.
- Upload reports after success or failure, with seven-day retention. Preserve the acceptance action's existing reporting.
- Record tested source revision, wheel checksum, actual compute type and loaded client/worker package versions. Include DQX, Python, Spark, MLflow, scikit-learn, SHAP, numba, coverage, NumPy, pandas, PyArrow and cloudpickle.
- If the client still receives an empty worker exception, use a targeted workspace reproduction to obtain worker-side diagnostics.

### Profiler repair

- Preserve the original candidates.
- Select the maximum ten features from candidates excluding the resolved grouping columns.
- Restore grouping-column types from the original candidates into the returned type map.
- Preserve existing treatment of omitted candidates beyond the feature limit.
- Keep the failing cardinality test unchanged; add assertions covering automatic grouping, explicit grouping, grouping opt-out and the feature limit.

### Collision repair

- Reproduce all four existing single/ensemble × driver/distributed variants, initially serially without test retries.
- Preserve the original map/double collision fields, duplicate rows, two consecutive checks and threshold zero.
- Diagnose differences through narrowly controlled variants: contributions on/off, one/two checks, and individual collision fields.
- If code or dependency provenance differs, correct the packaged installation or supported dependency constraints through the existing build/lock workflows. Do not copy test-only compatibility patches into production.
- If a scoring defect remains with matched environments, fix the implicated boundary and retain the original regression.
- Do not replace the existing distributed output-isolation design without evidence that it causes the failure.

The worker exception remains a diagnostic dependency. An unresolved traceback must remain an explicit blocker rather than prompting a speculative fix.

## 2. Audit and strengthen the test suite

Review every anomaly unit and integration module, plus related explanation and E2E tests. Maintain a behavior-to-test inventory recording execution mode, dependencies, assertions and remaining gaps.

### Unit tests

- Replace the 22 literal-only validation tests with parameterized calls to production validators or public entry points. Cover meaningful boundaries, invalid types, empty values and non-finite numbers where applicable.
- Remove outdated registry-name and segmentation assumptions.
- Replace explainer tests that merely assert a non-null Spark expression with integration tests that materialize the result and prove severity precedence, threshold behavior and contributor selection.
- Reduce internal constructor/call-order patching in affected registry and workflow tests. Prefer existing injection seams, autospec dependencies and shared fixtures; add only small internal seams when necessary.
- Keep real Spark execution in integration tests. Move genuinely pure tests out of workspace-backed suites.
- Preserve strong numerical tests using independent expectations, deterministic fixtures, real estimators and justified tolerances.

### Integration assertions

- Assert expected row coverage and score nullability before computing quality metrics. Explicitly account for intentionally unscored unseen groups.
- Replace comparisons that truncate unequal results or silently skip null scores.
- Check threshold monotonicity using nested sets of flagged row identities, not counts alone.
- Use controlled perturbations to verify meaningful top contributors.
- Assert contributions through the public nested output, including disabled behavior.
- Compare row multisets or stable identities with duplicate counts; never rely on incidental Spark collection order.

Validate sensitivity with a small disposable-checkout exercise: deliberately break validation, grouping-type preservation and scorer output restoration, and confirm the relevant tests fail. Do not introduce a general mutation-testing framework.

## 3. Establish explicit production-path coverage

Use this compact scoring matrix on both classic and serverless CI:

| Model configuration | Execution | Contributions |
|---|---|---|
| Distribution, single model | Driver and distributed | Off and on |
| Distribution, ensemble | Driver and distributed | Off and on |
| Correlation, single model | Driver and distributed | Off and on |

Train once per model configuration within the appropriate fixture lifetime and score the same persisted model through both execution modes. Verify correlation's documented single-model resolution separately.
Every matrix case must check original values/schema, duplicate preservation, finite supported scores, severity/verdict parity, contribution contracts and absence of leaked temporary columns.
Use representative cases—not a full Cartesian expansion—for grouping, time conditioning, combined conditioning, mixed categories, nulls, unseen groups, row filters, multiple checks, repeated application, column reordering and partition changes. Include targeted ensemble confidence-standard-deviation coverage and metadata/programmatic API parity.
Add a maintained packaged-artifact test that trains/registers, starts a fresh process or session, reloads and scores using the exact built wheel. Include contribution-enabled distributed scoring in E2E and record the actual compute used.

### AI tests

- Separate numerical correlation, persistence and attribution checks from live endpoint availability.
- Extend the existing real-Spark tests that substitute only the serving boundary: complete-prompt redaction, response formats, malformed/null responses, grouping limits and row preservation.
- Keep live explanation smoke small and separately identified.
- Skip only recognized unavailable-endpoint conditions; unexpected application failures remain failures.
- Rename unsupported one-call-per-group claims or instrument the actual boundary. Constructed expressions and identical narratives do not establish request counts.

## 4. CI, resource ownership and acceptance

- Preserve first-attempt and retry outcomes separately. Disable blanket retries in manual deterministic regression runs; retain only narrowly classified, bounded infrastructure retries.
- Keep manual and CI suite selection aligned. A retry-success result must not conceal a first-attempt deterministic regression.
- Enable branch coverage consistently and publish available coverage after failures without replacing the original failure.
- Distinguish client coverage from remote-worker execution evidence. Add remote coverage only through supported collection mechanisms; do not claim it exists based on client coverage.
- Investigate Codecov failures without weakening thresholds or adding exclusions to obtain green checks.
- Correct the anomaly merge-queue gate while preserving fork and secret-access protections.
- Register cleanup immediately after acquiring resources, including before training. Retain factory-based ownership and report unresolved test-owned resources.

Acceptance sequence:
1. Reproduce the original failures and preserve diagnostics.
2. Pass the targeted repairs and strengthened tests.
3. Run make fmt, make test, then make docs-build on the final source tree.
4. Pass the complete classic/serverless anomaly suites, related explanation integration tests and packaged E2E coverage.
5. Reconcile every planned behavior with actual results, including skips, retries, cleanup failures and coverage limitations.

## 5. Execution limits and handoff

Manual Databricks diagnosis and verification have a four-hour wall-clock window from the first manual submission, with at most two concurrent STANDARD-serverless tasks and ten unique live narrative groups across the campaign. Disable AI elsewhere. Existing full CI suites retain their configured limits.
Use synthetic inputs and uniquely owned resources. At the deadline, stop only run-owned tasks, export available evidence and perform exact-resource cleanup. Report incomplete checks explicitly; do not extend the budget automatically.
Keep fixes, unit-test improvements, integration contracts and CI changes in focused commits. Run formatting before every commit, GPG-sign every commit and verify its signature.
Handoff includes signed commit hashes, the behavior-to-test inventory, exact source/artifact identities, first-attempt results, remaining limitations and cleanup outcomes. Implementation ends with reviewable local changes and evidence; pushing or posting reviewer replies is a separate step.
